thread:
jordan: Customer reported streaming responses hang when a transient network blip occurs. After the connection resumes we sometimes see a 0-byte data frame from a proxy and the Go SDK consumer stops emitting tokens. Repro attached, happens reproducibly for one customer. @maria can you triage?

maria: Ok. Do we have the go-sdk version and the minimal repro code? Also OS/kernel and whether they're going through a cloud LB? :eyes:

jordan: They are on go-sdk v0.10.3, go1.20, running app in k8s behind our gateway (envoy). Logs show repeated:
```
# log snippet
stream reader: got chunk len=0
scanner.Scan() returned false, no error
client-side stream loop stopped, not reconnecting
```
Minimal client pattern they use:
```go
resp, err := client.Stream(ctx, req)
if err != nil { return err }
scanner := bufio.NewScanner(resp.Body)
for scanner.Scan() {
    fmt.Println(scanner.Text())
}
if err := scanner.Err(); err != nil {
    log.Printf("stream error: %v", err)
}
```

maria: Thanks. Can you try to reproduce locally with curl/nghttp to confirm the zero-length frame? Also what does the server send in that spot — is it a keepalive flush or an end-of-chunk?

jordan: Reproduced locally hitting staging via our LB. curl --http2 -v shows a DATA frame with 0 bytes immediately after a normal data frame. The Go net/http stack surfaces this as a Read that returns 0, nil for that Read call; bufio.Scanner sees no token and Scan() returns false without an error, so the client code exits the loop. Proxy is envoy with HTTP/2 connection health pings.

ryan: This matches a known edge-case: bufio.Scanner is not signaled on a 0-byte Read when err==nil, so user loops that rely on Scan() can stop if they expect newline-delimited JSON and the underlying transport performs a zero-byte flush. Two immediate options: 1) Use json.Decoder on the resp.Body to decode token-by-token (Decoder handles partial reads better), or 2) upgrade to go-sdk v0.12.1 — we backported a fix where the streaming helper wraps resp.Body with a Reader that hides zero-length reads so scanners continue.

maria: Can you paste the minimal wrapper we used for the patch? I can give it to the customer as a temporary workaround.

ryan: Sure — workaround to swallow transient zero-length reads:
```go
// nonZeroReader wraps an io.Reader and retries on 0-byte reads with nil error.
type nonZeroReader struct{ r io.Reader }
func (n nonZeroReader) Read(p []byte) (int, error) {
    for {
        nread, err := n.r.Read(p)
        if nread == 0 && err == nil {
            // transient zero-length read from http2 frame, loop and try again
            continue
        }
        return nread, err
    }
}
// usage:
// scanner := bufio.NewScanner(nonZeroReader{resp.Body})
```

jordan: Tried that locally and it fixes the hang — scanner keeps returning tokens. Customer confirmed the same.

maria: Great. For the long term we'll push SDK v0.12.1 to customers. Ryan, can we also add a small unit test that injects a fake Reader that returns 0, nil intermittently to prevent regressions?

ryan: Yep adding test now. Also adding a note in changelog about envoy/HTTP2 zero-length data frame behavior.

deploy-bot: deploy queued for go-sdk v0.12.1 -> canary (1/3) :package:

maria: Thanks team — I've communicated the workaround to the customer, provided the nonZeroReader snippet and the recommendation to upgrade to v0.12.1 when it lands. Marking this as resolved from support side, will follow up if customer sees anything after the upgrade. :thumbsup:
